 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Your new city street is flat asphalt, wall to wall. Then the first storm hits.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The water has nowhere to go. It sits there, and the cars start floating.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you build the road higher in the middle, with a curb on each side.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Just ten centimeters of crown, and the rain runs straight to the gutters.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But the gutters carry all of it downhill, and the lowest corner floods.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you cut storm drains into the curb on every block.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And under the street, a pipe carries the water away to the river.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then a real storm comes, and that little pipe fills up in minutes.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The pressure builds until the manhole cover shoots into the air.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you dig a pipe almost as tall as a person.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And you tilt it downhill, so gravity moves the water with no pumps at all.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now the street stays dry, even in a downpour.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "For the biggest storms, some cities hide giant tunnels even deeper.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why every curb in the world has a little slot in it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just built a storm drain. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
